To determine how much water Jasmine would drink in the next 10 miles, we need to first find her rate of drinking water per mile. We know that she drank 1.5 pints of water in 3 miles, so her rate is:

{eq}1.5 \text{ pints} / 3 \text{ miles} = 0.5 \text{ pints per mile}

{/eq}

This means that for every mile she hikes, she drinks 0.5 pints of water. To find out how much water she would drink in 10 miles, we can multiply her rate by the number of miles:

{eq}0.5 \text{ pints per mile} \times 10 \text{ miles} = 5 \text{ pints}

{/eq}

Therefore, Jasmine would drink 5 pints of water in the next 10 miles.

